Hypothesis: `status` carries three unrelated claims through two values.
Outcome: CONFIRMED, one leg REFUTED, and the sharpest finding was one the
hypothesis did not predict.

ENFORCED vs CONSUMED. _validate_catalog_dict checks exactly one thing — that
`complete` carries an allowlisted stdlib_provenance.source_url. But two
consumers read it as a COVERAGE claim: io_boundary.py:1747
(dst_classification_unreliable, surfaced at cli.py:4937) and :1267. Test 1
fires completely — 14/14 shipped catalogues, status=='complete' <=> provenance
present, zero divergence, so the enforced content restates
`stdlib_provenance is not None`. Test 2 fires: `complete` is both the maximal
explicit claim and what you get by saying nothing.

THE VALUE THE HYPOTHESIS MISSED. Inventory turned up a THIRD input value,
`status: overlay`, checked at :1137 and erased at :1174 before the object is
built — a door password, not a state (Tests 3+4 both fire; verdict FOLD). The
five-shape producer trace found zero stored producers, with shape 5 finding
only the erasure site itself.

THE SHARPEST FINDING, THE REVERSE OF WHAT I PREDICTED. `status` does not gate
the one thing that grants confirmability: module_io_is_enumerated reads
stdlib_module_completeness and never consults status. python is `complete`
with 1 audited module, rust `complete` with 0, erlang `complete` with 0.

LIVE SILENT BUG. erlang declares `complete` on an erlang.org URL while
carrying ZERO subprocess rows and filing its shell-out os:cmd under env_read,
so dst_classification_unreliable is False for every Erlang chain (WI-jupaf).

LEG 3 REFUTED, recorded rather than inflated. The missing-file `complete` is
not smuggling: load_catalog returns is_supported=False and status merely hits
its dataclass default. The "exists" question has its own field and consumers
check it. Kept with a written re-evaluation trigger.

No new axis declared. Two items filed: WI-maduh (document enforced-vs-gating),
